    </ol>
	</objectives>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-no-linear-term">
    <title>Special case: no linear term</title>
    <p>
      The last section gave a general method for solving quadratic equations using the method of factoring.
      We now introduce a method that works in the special case that our quadratic equation doesn't have a linear term.
      As a reminder, in general, the kinds of terms that can appear are quadratic, linear, and constant.
      A quadratic term has <m>x^2</m>, 
      while a linear term has <m>x</m> to the first power (with the power usually omitted), 
      and a constant term is a term that does not have the variable at all.
      For example, <m>8x^2 + 7x - 60=0</m> has a quadratic term, linear term, and constant term.
      By contrast, <m>8x^2-60=0</m> has a quadratic term and a constant term, but no linear term.

      <!-- Strategy -->
      <computation xml:id="quadratic-no-linear-term">
        <title>Solving a quadratic equation with no linear term</title>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <alert>When does this strategy apply?</alert> 
                This strategy applies in any equation we are asked to solve a quadratic equation that does not have a linear term.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <alert>How to apply the strategy</alert>
                <ol>
                  <li>
                    <p>
                      Step 1: add and subtract on both sides so that the quadratic term and constant term appear on opposite sides of the equation.
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <p>
                      Step 2: square root both sides. (The first step in which square rooting occurs on both sides needs to have the plus-or-minus sign. While this symbol can be placed on either side, it is more convenient to put this on the side that does not have the variable.)
                    </p>
                  </li>
                </ol>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </computation>
      
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Solve the equation <m>x^2-9=0</m>.          
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            Since the equation has no linear term, we can apply <xref ref="quadratic-no-linear-term"/>.
            Adding <m>9</m> to both sides, <me>x^2=9.</me>
            Step 2 of the strategy tells us to square root both sides and introduce a plus-or-minus sign: <me>\sqrt{x^2} = \pm \sqrt{9}.</me>             
            Note, it would be slightly incorrect if before aforementioned equation we had written <me>\sqrt{x^2} = \sqrt{9}.</me>
            From <m>\sqrt{x^2} = \pm \sqrt{9}</m>, we simplify each side independently to get <m>x= \pm3</m>.           
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
      Getting our final answer to be <m>x=\pm3</m> (which is read aloud "<m>x</m> equals plus or minus <m>3</m>") is equivalent to the final answer 
      <m>x=3 \text{ or } x = -3</m> given earlier in <xref refid="solve-xsquared-minus9equals0"/>.
    </p>

    <principle>
      <title>Expectation when square rooting both sides of an equation</title>
      <p>
        In the very first step in which the action of square rooting happens on both sides of an equation (even in the case when the action is indicated in the previous step's work), 
        a plus-or-minus sign must appear on one side or other side of the equation.
      </p>
    </principle>

    <warning>
      <p>
        A square root sign occurs when square rooting both sides of an <em>equation</em>,
        but when simplify an <em>expression</em> that has a square root symbol, a plus-or-minus sign is never artificially introduced into the problem.
      </p>
    </warning>
    <p>
      For example, when we simplified <m>\sqrt{18}</m> in <xref ref="simplify-sqrt18"/>, we never wrote the <m>\pm</m> symbol.
    </p>
    <p>
      To be sure that we understand the concepts that we have introduced, let's answer the following questions:
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Simplify <m>\sqrt{20}</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>\sqrt{20}=\sqrt{4 \cdot 5} = \sqrt{4} \cdot \sqrt{5} = 2\sqrt{5}</m>
          </p>
          <p>
            We never performed the action of square rooting both sides of an equation, so that's why there is no plus-or-minus sign anywhere in our work.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>

      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Solve <m>x^2 + 1= 20</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            Subtracting <m>1</m> on both sides, <me>x^2=19.</me>
            Square rooting both sides, <me>\sqrt{x^2} = \pm \sqrt{19}</me> which simplifies to <me>x = \pm \sqrt{19}</me>
          </p>
        </answer>
        <answer>
          <p>
            Subtracting <m>1</m> on both sides, <me>x^2=19.</me>
            Square rooting both sides, <me>x = \pm \sqrt{19}.</me>
            In this answer, did not show the step that indicates the square rooting on both sides: just the result.
          </p>
        </answer>
        <answer>
          <p>
            We provide an answer without commentary:
            <me>x^2 + 1 = 20</me>
            <me>x^2 = 19</me>
            <me>\sqrt{x^2} = \pm\sqrt{19}</me>
            <me>x = \pm \sqrt{19}</me>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>

      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find the error in this written work:
            <me>x^2 + 1 = 20</me>
            <me>\sqrt{x^2} = \sqrt{19}</me>
            <me>x = \pm \sqrt{19}</me>            
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            There are three equations. The middle equaton looks like someone first had written <m>x^2=19</m> but then added work on top of that line by writing in square roots on both sides, but without having a plus-or-minus sign on that line of work.
            The <em>first</em> line of writing that has square rooting on both sides needs the plus-or-minus sign.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Solve <m>(x-6)^2=19</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            Square rooting both sides, <me>x-6 = \pm \sqrt{19}</me>
            Adding <m>6</m> to both sides, <me>x = 6 \pm \sqrt{19}</me>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>


      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Solve <m>(x-6)^2=19</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            Square rooting both sides, <me>x-6 = \pm \sqrt{19}</me>
            Adding <m>6</m> to both sides, <me>x = 6 \pm \sqrt{19}</me>
          </p>
        </answer>        
      </example>

      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Solve <m>(x+3)^2=5</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            Square rooting both sides, <me>x+3 = \pm \sqrt{5}</me>
            Adding <m>-3</m> to both sides, <me>x = -3 \pm \sqrt{5}</me>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>

    </p>

  </subsection>

	<subsection>
		<title>Completing the square of the area model</title>

    <p>
      Recall from <xref ref="geometric-addition"/> that addition is represented by gluing sticks together, 
      and from <xref ref="geometric-multiplication"/> that multiplication is represented by the area of a rectangle.
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Geometrically justify <m>(a+b)(c+d)=ac+ad+bc+bd</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            The left side represents the area of a rectangle with sides <m>(a+b)</m> and <m>(c+d)</m>.
            <figure>
              <caption>Area model figure</caption>
              <image width="30%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3);
                      \draw (2,0) -- (2,3);
                      \draw (0,1.5) -- (4,1.5);
                      \node at (1,2.25) {$ac$};
                      \node at (3,0.75) {$ad$};
                      \node at (3, 2.25) {$bc$}; % upper right area
                      \node at (1,0.75) {$bd$}; % lower left area
                      %
                      \node at (-.5,2.25) {$a$};
                      \node at (1,3.25) {$b$};
                      \node at (-.5,0.75) {$c$}; % 2nd vertical side
                      \node at (3,3.25) {$d$}; % 2nd horizontal side
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
            </figure>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>
    


